From 3e6ecfd35b9bf35c5014734291edd53c3b37a52f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Life OS Dev <lifeos@local>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 19:26:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(test): repair media importer test comment syntax
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

GitHub Actions run 36128704698, :app:compileDebugUnitTestKotlin reported:

  MediaStoreImporterTest.kt:73: Missing '}'
  MediaStoreImporterTest.kt:275: Unclosed comment

Root cause is one KDoc line. Kotlin block comments nest, so the two adjacent
characters /* inside the prose at line 79 opened a nested block comment. Nothing
closed it, so every following line — including the closing brace at line 274 —
was swallowed as comment, which is why the two errors surfaced as an apparently
unrelated brace/EOF pair 200 lines apart.

The text referred to the MIME fallback in MediaStoreImporter. The wildcard is now
written as `image/<wildcard>`, which cannot open a comment. Wording, meaning and
the surrounding explanation are unchanged.

Main-source occurrences of the literal wildcard are inside string literals
(picker calls and the MediaStoreImporter fallback itself), where /* is not a
comment opener; they are untouched, and no importer logic changed.

Verified by a state-machine scan of all 142 .kt files under app/src that reports
nesting block-comment openers in comment bodies and files ending inside a
comment: 0 risk files, 0 unclosed comments.
---
 .../java/com/qq/closie/life/media/MediaStoreImporterTest.kt     | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/app/src/test/java/com/qq/closie/life/media/MediaStoreImporterTest.kt b/app/src/test/java/com/qq/closie/life/media/MediaStoreImporterTest.kt
index 022b64f..b0108df 100644
--- a/app/src/test/java/com/qq/closie/life/media/MediaStoreImporterTest.kt
+++ b/app/src/test/java/com/qq/closie/life/media/MediaStoreImporterTest.kt
@@ -76,7 +76,7 @@ class MediaStoreImporterTest {
      * The extension the importer derives for a `file://` source.
      *
      * `ContentResolver.getType` returns null for a `file://` URI, so the importer falls back to
-     * `image/*` and then to its `else -> "img"` branch. Hard-coding `.png` here would make the tests
+     * `image/<wildcard>` and then to its `else -> "img"` branch. Hard-coding `.png` here would make the tests
      * assert against a name production never creates — the assertions would fail for the right reason
      * but point at the wrong cause, and a future change to the fallback would silently decouple these
      * tests from reality. Naming the constant makes that coupling explicit.
